\label{ch:runtime-org}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:runtime-org-intro}

A compiled program does not exist in isolation. When the operating system loads an executable, it establishes a runtime environment: a virtual address space partitioned into segments for code, data, heap, and stack; a set of initialization routines; and a collection of runtime services. The compiler must generate code that conforms to this environment, and for many languages, must also provide a runtime library that implements higher-level services such as memory management, exception handling, and concurrency.

This chapter examines the organization of a program's runtime environment, from the layout of its address space to the details of stack frames, heap management, and dynamic loading. Understanding runtime organization is essential for compiler developers, who must generate code that interacts correctly with the operating system and runtime library.

\section{Program Segments}\label{sec:program-segments}

\subsection{The Address Space Layout}\label{sec:address-space}

A typical process address space on a modern 64-bit system is divided into the following segments:

