\subsection{Text Segment}\label{sec:text-segment}

The text segment contains the executable instructions. It is typically:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Read-only}: the OS maps it with read and execute permissions but not write.
  \item \textbf{Shareable}: multiple processes running the same program can share the same physical pages.
  \item \textbf{Position-dependent or independent}: depending on whether the executable is relocatable.
\end{itemize}

The compiler places all generated code in the text segment. Constant data (string literals, literal pools) may also be placed here or in a separate read-only data segment.

\subsection{Data Segment}\label{sec:data-segment}

The data segment contains initialized global and static variables. It is:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Readable and writable}: the program modifies these values during execution.
  \item \textbf{Initialized from the executable}: the initial values are stored in the object file and loaded by the OS.
  \item \textbf{Copy-on-write}: if the executable is shared, modifications create private copies of the affected pages.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{BSS Segment}\label{sec:bss-segment}

The BSS (Block Started by Symbol) segment contains uninitialized global and static variables. By convention, these are zero-initialized. The BSS segment:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Does not occupy space in the executable file (only its size is recorded).
  \item Is zero-filled by the OS loader at program startup.
  \item Reduces executable file size for programs with large uninitialized arrays.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Heap}\label{sec:heap-overview}

The heap is the region from which dynamic memory is allocated (via \texttt{malloc}, \texttt{new}, etc.). It grows upward (toward higher addresses) as memory is allocated. The heap's organization is discussed in detail in \cref{ch:memory-management,ch:garbage-collection}.

\subsection{Stack}\label{sec:stack-overview}

The stack grows downward (toward lower addresses) and is used for function call management. Each function call creates a new stack frame containing local variables, saved registers, and the return address. The stack's structure is discussed in \cref{sec:stack-frames}.

\section{Stack Frames and Activation Records}\label{sec:stack-frames}

\subsection{Anatomy of a Stack Frame}\label{sec:frame-anatomy}

A stack frame (activation record) contains all the state associated with a single function invocation:

\begin{definition}[Activation Record]\label{def:activation-record}
An \emph{activation record} is the collection of data associated with a single invocation of a procedure. It includes:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The return address
  \item The control link (pointer to the caller's frame)
  \item The arguments (if passed on the stack)
  \item Local variables
  \item Saved register values
  \item Temporaries
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Prologue and Epilogue}\label{sec:prologue-epilogue}

The function \emph{prologue} sets up the stack frame; the \emph{epilogue} tears it down.

\begin{example}[x86-64 Prologue and Epilogue]\label{ex:prologue-epilogue}
A typical x86-64 function prologue:
\begin{verbatim}
  push rbp              ; save caller's frame pointer
  mov  rbp, rsp         ; set up frame pointer
  sub  rsp, N           ; allocate local variable space
  mov  [rbp - off], rbx ; save callee-saved registers
\end{verbatim}
The corresponding epilogue:
\begin{verbatim}
  mov  rbx, [rbp - off] ; restore callee-saved registers
  mov  rsp, rbp         ; deallocate locals
  pop  rbp              ; restore caller's frame pointer
  ret                   ; return to caller
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Leaf Function Optimization}\label{sec:leaf-optimization}

A \emph{leaf function} (one that calls no other functions) can often avoid setting up a full stack frame:
\begin{itemize}
  \item No need to save the frame pointer.
  \item Can use the red zone (x86-64: 128 bytes below SP) for local storage.
  \item May not need to adjust the stack pointer at all.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Variable-Size Stack Frames}\label{sec:variable-frames}

Some languages support variable-size stack allocations (C's \texttt{alloca}, VLAs). These require:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Dynamic adjustment of the stack pointer at runtime.
  \item A frame pointer to maintain a stable reference point.
  \item Special handling in the epilogue (cannot simply restore SP from FP if the frame size is dynamic).
\end{itemize}

\section{Heap Organization}\label{sec:heap-org}

\subsection{The Allocator Interface}\label{sec:allocator-interface}

The heap allocator provides:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{malloc(size)}: allocate \texttt{size} bytes, returning a pointer.
  \item \texttt{free(ptr)}: deallocate the block pointed to by \texttt{ptr}.
  \item \texttt{realloc(ptr, size)}: resize an allocated block.
  \item \texttt{calloc(n, size)}: allocate and zero-initialize.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Allocation Strategies}\label{sec:allocation-strategies}

Heap allocators use various strategies:

\begin{definition}[Allocation Strategy]\label{def:alloc-strategy}
Common allocation strategies include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{First fit}: scan from the beginning, use the first block large enough.
  \item \textbf{Best fit}: find the smallest block that is large enough.
  \item \textbf{Next fit}: like first fit, but continue from where the last allocation ended.
  \item \textbf{Segregated free lists}: maintain separate lists for different size classes.
  \item \textbf{Slab allocation}: pre-allocate fixed-size objects for common sizes.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{The Runtime's Role}\label{sec:runtime-heap}

For languages with garbage collection, the runtime system provides the heap allocator as part of the GC infrastructure. The allocator must:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Provide fast allocation (often a bump pointer in the nursery).
  \item Cooperate with the garbage collector (write barriers, allocation triggers).
  \item Handle large objects specially (direct allocation to old generation).
\end{itemize}

\section{Runtime Initialization}\label{sec:runtime-init}

\subsection{Startup Sequence}\label{sec:startup-sequence}

When a program starts, the following sequence occurs:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item The OS loader maps the executable and its shared libraries into memory.
  \item The dynamic linker resolves symbols and performs relocations.
  \item Control is transferred to the \emph{entry point} (typically \texttt{\_start}).
  \item The C runtime initialization code (\texttt{\_start} in \texttt{crt0.o}):
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Sets up the initial stack (argc, argv, envp).
      \item Calls constructor functions (\texttt{.init\_array} / \texttt{.ctors}).
      \item Calls \texttt{main()}.
      \item Calls \texttt{exit()} with \texttt{main}'s return value.
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Constructor and Destructor Functions}\label{sec:ctors-dtors}

Many languages need to run initialization code before \texttt{main} and cleanup code after:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{C/C++}: global constructors (C++ static objects), \texttt{\_\_attribute\_\_((constructor))}.
  \item \textbf{Java}: static initializers, class loading.
  \item \textbf{Rust}: \texttt{std::rt::lang\_start} sets up the runtime before calling \texttt{main}.
\end{itemize}

The compiler emits constructor/destructor references in special sections (\texttt{.init\_array}, \texttt{.fini\_array}) that the runtime iterates over during startup/shutdown.

\subsection{Language Runtime Initialization}\label{sec:lang-runtime-init}

Languages with rich runtime systems perform additional initialization:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Garbage collector}: initialize the heap, set up GC threads.
  \item \textbf{Thread system}: initialize the thread scheduler.
  \item \textbf{Exception handling}: set up unwind tables.
  \item \textbf{Module system}: initialize module state.
  \item \textbf{I/O system}: initialize standard streams.
\end{itemize}

\section{Thread-Local Storage}\label{sec:tls}

\subsection{The Need for Thread-Local Data}\label{sec:tls-need}

In multithreaded programs, each thread needs its own copy of certain variables (e.g., \texttt{errno} in C, the current exception in Java). Thread-local storage (TLS) provides this.

\begin{definition}[Thread-Local Storage]\label{def:tls}
\emph{Thread-local storage} (TLS) is a mechanism by which each thread in a process has its own instance of a variable. Accessing a TLS variable yields the calling thread's instance.
\end{definition}

\subsection{TLS Implementation Models}\label{sec:tls-models}

On ELF systems, TLS is implemented through several models:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{TLS access models (ELF)}
\label{tab:tls-models}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Mechanism} & \textbf{Use Case} \\
\hline
General Dynamic & Runtime call to \texttt{tls\_get\_addr} & Shared libraries, dynamic loading \\
Local Dynamic & One runtime call, multiple offsets & Multiple TLS vars in one module \\
Initial Exec & Fixed offset from TP register & Executable, loaded at startup \\
Local Exec & Direct offset from TP register & Variable in executable \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The \emph{thread pointer} (TP) is a register that points to the current thread's TLS block:
\begin{itemize}
  \item x86-64: \texttt{FS} segment register (the TP is at \texttt{FS:0}).
  \item ARM64: \texttt{TPIDR\_EL0} system register.
  \item RISC-V: \texttt{tp} (\texttt{x4}) register.
\end{itemize}

\section{Dynamic Loading and Symbol Resolution}\label{sec:dynamic-loading}

\subsection{Dynamic Loading}\label{sec:dynamic-loading-mech}

Programs can load shared libraries at runtime using \texttt{dlopen()} (POSIX) or \texttt{LoadLibrary()} (Windows). This enables:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Plugin architectures.
  \item Lazy loading of optional features.
  \item Hot-swapping of implementations.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Symbol Resolution at Runtime}\label{sec:runtime-symbol-resolution}

When a shared library is loaded, the dynamic linker:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Maps the library's segments into memory.
  \item Performs relocations (adjusting PC-relative and GOT entries).
  \item Resolves imported symbols (possibly loading additional libraries).
  \item Runs the library's initialization functions.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Symbol Interposition}\label{sec:symbol-interposition}

In the ELF model, a symbol defined in an executable or an earlier-loaded library can \emph{interpose} (override) a symbol with the same name in a later-loaded library. This enables:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{LD\_PRELOAD}: loading a library before all others to override functions (used for profiling, debugging, security).
  \item \textbf{Weak symbols}: library functions that can be overridden by the application.
\end{itemize}

The compiler must be aware of interposition when optimizing: a call to a function in a shared library cannot be inlined or devirtualized if the symbol might be interposed.

\section{Runtime Support for Language Features}\label{sec:runtime-support}

\subsection{Reflection and Introspection}\label{sec:reflection}

Languages with reflection (Java, C\#, Python) require runtime data structures that describe types, methods, fields, and annotations. The compiler emits \emph{metadata} alongside the code:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Type descriptors}: structures describing each type's layout, methods, and interfaces.
  \item \textbf{Method tables}: virtual dispatch tables (vtables) mapping method names to implementations.
  \item \textbf{Field maps}: descriptions of object layouts for garbage collection and serialization.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Dynamic Dispatch}\label{sec:dynamic-dispatch}

Object-oriented languages use dynamic dispatch to select the implementation of a method at runtime. Common implementation strategies include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Virtual method tables (vtables)}: each object contains a pointer to a table of method implementations. Dispatch is a double indirection: object $\to$ vtable $\to$ method.
  \item \textbf{Inline caches}: cache the result of the last dispatch for each call site, exploiting the observation that most call sites are monomorphic.
  \item \textbf{Dispatch trees}: for languages with multiple dispatch (e.g., Common Lisp), more complex data structures are needed.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Closures and Environments}\label{sec:closures-runtime}

Languages with first-class closures must represent closures at runtime:

\begin{definition}[Closure Representation]\label{def:closure-rep}
A \emph{closure} is a pair $(code, env)$ where $code$ is a pointer to the compiled function and $env$ is an \emph{environment} containing the values of the free variables captured by the closure.
\end{definition}

The environment may be represented as:
\begin{itemize}
  \item A fixed-size record (when the set of free variables is known at compile time).
  \item A hash map (for dynamic languages).
  \item A linked chain of frames (for statically-scoped languages with nested functions).
\end{itemize}

\section{Signal Handling and the Runtime}\label{sec:signals}

\subsection{Signals and the Runtime}\label{sec:signals-runtime}

Operating system signals (SIGSEGV, SIGINT, SIGFPE, etc.) interact with the runtime in important ways:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Segmentation faults}: the runtime may install a signal handler to implement garbage collection (for conservative collectors), stack overflow detection, or null pointer detection.
  \item \textbf{Floating-point exceptions}: the runtime may configure the FPU to trap or ignore certain exceptions.
  \item \textbf{Interrupts}: SIGINT (Ctrl+C) is typically handled by the runtime to support clean shutdown.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Signal-Safe Code}\label{sec:signal-safe}

Code that runs in signal handlers must be signal-safe: it cannot use locks, allocate memory, or call non-reentrant functions. The compiler's runtime must be designed with this constraint in mind.

\section{Profiling Support}\label{sec:profiling-support}

\subsection{Runtime Profiling Infrastructure}\label{sec:profiling-infra}

The runtime provides infrastructure for profiling:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Sampling profilers}: the OS periodically interrupts the process and records the call stack. The runtime must provide stack unwinding information (via \texttt{.eh\_frame} or frame pointers).
  \item \textbf{Instrumenting profilers}: the compiler inserts profiling code at function entry/exit and basic block boundaries.
  \item \textbf{Hardware performance counters}: the runtime provides interfaces for reading CPU performance counters (cache misses, branch mispredictions, etc.).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Stack Unwinding for Profilers}\label{sec:unwinding-profiling}

Profilers need to walk the call stack. This requires:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Frame pointer chains (if the frame pointer is not omitted).
  \item DWARF \texttt{.eh\_frame} information (for frame-pointer-omitted code).
  \item JIT-compiled code must register its unwind information with the runtime.
\end{itemize}

\section{Code Signing and Security}\label{sec:code-signing}

\subsection{Runtime Security}\label{sec:runtime-security}

Modern runtimes must consider security:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Code signing}: on platforms like iOS and Android, all code must be signed. The runtime verifies signatures before execution.
  \item \textbf{W\^{}X policy}: memory pages must not be both writable and executable simultaneously. JIT compilers must use \texttt{mprotect} to switch between writing and executing.
  \item \textbf{Stack canaries}: the runtime inserts canary values on the stack to detect buffer overflows.
  \item \textbf{ASLR}: the runtime supports address space layout randomization by using position-independent code.
\end{itemize}

