\section{Advanced Thread-Local Storage}\label{sec:advanced-tls}

Thread-local storage is a critical mechanism for modern multithreaded programs. Beyond the basic models described earlier, TLS implementation involves several subtleties that compiler developers must understand.

\subsection{TLS Variable Layout}\label{sec:tls-layout}

Each thread's TLS block is organized as a contiguous region of memory. The layout depends on the TLS model and the number of TLS variables:

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  seg/.style={draw, minimum width=7cm, minimum height=0.5cm, font=\small, align=center},
]
  \node[seg, fill=TomeExBg] (dtv) at (0, 3) {DTV Pointer (Dynamic TLS Vector)};
  \node[seg, fill=TomeBg] (static) at (0, 2.2) {Static TLS Block (Initial Exec / Local Exec)};
  \node[seg, fill=TomeDefBg] (padding) at (0, 1.6) {Padding / Alignment};
  \node[seg, fill=TomeWarnBg] (tcb) at (0, 0.8) {Thread Control Block (TCB)};
  \node[seg, fill=TomeBg] (dtvblock) at (0, 0) {DTV Entries (for General/Local Dynamic)};
  
  \node[right=0.3cm of dtv, font=\small\itshape, text=TomeRule] {TP (Thread Pointer)};
  \node[right=0.3cm of tcb, font=\small\itshape, text=TomeRule] {TCB base};
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Thread-local storage block layout (Variant II, used by glibc on x86-64)}
\label{fig:tls-layout}
\end{figure}

In Variant II (used by glibc, musl, and most modern Unix systems), the thread pointer points to the end of the static TLS block, and offsets are negative. In Variant I (used by some older systems), the thread pointer points to the beginning, and offsets are positive.

\subsection{TLS Access Code Generation}\label{sec:tls-codegen}

The compiler generates different code sequences depending on the TLS model:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Local Exec (fastest, variable in main executable):
  //   mov %fs:var@tpoff, %eax
  
  // Initial Exec (variable in shared library loaded at startup):
  //   mov var@gottpoff(%rip), %rax
  //   mov %fs:(%rax), %eax
  
  // General Dynamic (most general, supports dlopen):
  //   lea var@tlsgd(%rip), %rdi
  //   call __tls_get_addr@plt
  //   mov (%rax), %eax
  
  // Local Dynamic (multiple vars in one module, one call):
  //   lea var1@tlsld(%rip), %rdi
  //   call __tls_get_addr@plt
  //   mov var1@dtpoff(%rax), %ebx   // first variable
  //   mov var2@dtpoff(%rax), %ecx   // second variable
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{TLS Optimization: Model Relaxation}\label{sec:tls-relaxation}

The linker can \emph{relax} TLS access sequences from more general to more efficient models when it determines that a less general model suffices:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{TLS model relaxation transitions}
\label{tab:tls-relaxation}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
\textbf{From} & \textbf{To} & \textbf{Condition} \\
\hline
General Dynamic & Local Dynamic & Variable is local (not preemptible) \\
General Dynamic & Initial Exec & Library loaded at startup \\
Local Dynamic & Initial Exec & Only one TLS variable in module \\
Initial Exec & Local Exec & Variable is in the executable \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The compiler can emit the most general model and rely on the linker to relax it, or it can use visibility attributes (\texttt{hidden}, \texttt{protected}) to indicate the appropriate model at compile time.

\subsection{TLS and the Garbage Collector}\label{sec:tls-gc}

For garbage-collected languages, TLS variables present a challenge: the GC must be able to find all TLS variables to scan them for root pointers. Solutions include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{TLS root sets}: each thread maintains a list of its TLS variable addresses, registered with the GC.
  \item \textbf{Conservative scanning}: the GC scans the entire TLS block for potential pointers.
  \item \textbf{Precise TLS maps}: the compiler generates metadata describing the layout of TLS variables, including which slots contain pointers.
\end{itemize}

