\section{Dynamic Loading in Depth}\label{sec:dynamic-loading-depth}

Dynamic loading is the mechanism by which shared libraries are loaded into a running process's address space. Understanding this mechanism is essential for compiler developers who must generate code that interacts with dynamically loaded libraries.

\subsection{The Dynamic Linking Process}\label{sec:dynamic-linking-process}

When a shared library is loaded (either at program startup or via \texttt{dlopen}), the dynamic linker performs the following steps:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Map segments.} The library's ELF segments are mapped into the process's address space using \texttt{mmap}.
  \item \textbf{Process dependencies.} The library's required libraries (recorded in \texttt{DT\_NEEDED} entries) are loaded recursively.
  \item \textbf{Assign addresses.} If the library is not position-independent, the dynamic linker assigns it a load address.
  \item \textbf{Perform relocations.} The library's relocation entries are processed, adjusting addresses to reflect the actual load address.
  \item \textbf{Resolve symbols.} Imported symbols are resolved by searching the global symbol table.
  \item \textbf{Initialize.} The library's initialization functions (\texttt{.init\_array}) are called.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Lazy Binding and the PLT}\label{sec:lazy-binding}

To minimize startup time, function calls to shared library functions use \emph{lazy binding}: the function's address is not resolved until the first call.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  block/.style={draw, fill=TomeBg, minimum width=2.5cm, minimum height=0.6cm, font=\small, align=center},
  arr/.style={->, thick, >=stealth}
]
  \node[block] (call) at (0, 3) {Call site};
  \node[block, fill=TomeExBg] (plt) at (0, 1.5) {PLT entry\\(jump stub)};
  \node[block, fill=TomeDefBg] (got) at (3, 1.5) {GOT entry\\(initially -> PLT+6)};
  \node[block, fill=TomeWarnBg] (plt0) at (0, 0) {PLT[0]\\(resolver stub)};
  \node[block, fill=TomeExBg] (resolver) at (3, 0) {Dynamic linker\\resolver};
  \node[block, fill=TomeBg] (target) at (6, 1.5) {Library function\\(actual code)};

  \draw[arr] (call) -- (plt);
  \draw[arr] (plt) -- (got);
  \draw[arr, dashed] (got) -- node[right, font=\tiny] {1st call} (plt0);
  \draw[arr] (plt0) -- (resolver);
  \draw[arr, dotted, blue] (resolver) -- node[below, font=\tiny] {resolves} (target);
  \draw[arr, dashed, red] (got.north) to[bend left] node[right, font=\tiny] {After resolution} (target.north);
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Lazy binding: PLT/GOT interaction during dynamic symbol resolution}
\label{fig:lazy-binding}
\end{figure}

The first time a function is called:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item The PLT entry jumps to the GOT entry.
  \item The GOT entry initially points back to PLT+6 (the resolver stub).
  \item The resolver pushes the symbol's relocation index and calls the dynamic linker.
  \item The dynamic linker resolves the symbol, writes the actual address to the GOT entry, and jumps to the function.
\end{enumerate}

Subsequent calls go directly through the GOT entry to the function, with no dynamic linker involvement.

\subsection{Position-Independent Code (PIC)}\label{sec:pic}

Shared libraries must be compiled as \emph{position-independent code} (PIC) so they can be loaded at any address:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Global Offset Table (GOT).} All global variable accesses go through the GOT, which is relocated at load time.
  \item \textbf{Procedure Linkage Table (PLT).} All function calls to external symbols go through the PLT.
  \item \textbf{PC-relative addressing.} Modern x86-64 uses RIP-relative addressing to access the GOT, avoiding the need for a dedicated register.
\end{itemize}

\begin{verbatim}
  // Non-PIC (absolute addressing):
  mov global_var, %eax       // Direct absolute address
  
  // PIC (x86-64, RIP-relative):
  mov global_var@GOTPCREL(%rip), %rax   // Load GOT entry
  mov (%rax), %eax                       // Load through GOT
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Symbol Visibility and Interposition}\label{sec:symbol-visibility}

The compiler controls symbol visibility through attributes:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Symbol visibility levels (ELF)}
\label{tab:symbol-visibility}
\begin{tabular}{llp{5cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Visibility} & \textbf{Attribute} & \textbf{Effect} \\
\hline
Default & (none) & Symbol is visible and can be interposed \\
Hidden & \texttt{\_\_attribute\_\_((visibility("hidden")))} & Symbol is not exported; cannot be interposed \\
Protected & \texttt{\_\_attribute\_\_((visibility("protected")))} & Symbol is exported but references bind to the definition in this library \\
Internal & \texttt{\_\_attribute\_\_((visibility("internal")))} & Like hidden, but not available to other components \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Hidden visibility enables more aggressive optimization: the compiler can inline functions, devirtualize calls, and perform constant propagation on global variables that are not visible to other libraries.

