\section{Runtime Initialization in Depth}\label{sec:runtime-init-depth}

The initialization of a program's runtime environment is a complex process that varies significantly across languages and platforms.

\subsection{The Startup Sequence in Detail}\label{sec:startup-detail}

On a modern Linux system, the complete startup sequence is:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item The kernel creates a process and maps the executable.
  \item The kernel maps the \emph{virtual dynamic shared object} (vDSO) for fast system calls.
  \item The kernel transfers control to the dynamic linker (\texttt{ld-linux-x86-64.so}).
  \item The dynamic linker reads the executable's \texttt{PT\_INTERP} segment to find itself.
  \item The dynamic linker maps all required shared libraries.
  \item The dynamic linker performs symbol resolution and relocations.
  \item The dynamic linker calls the executable's entry point (\texttt{\_start}).
  \item \texttt{\_start} (in \texttt{crt1.o}):
  \begin{itemize}
    \item Sets up the initial stack frame (\texttt{argc}, \texttt{argv}, \texttt{envp}).
    \item Calls \texttt{\_\_libc\_start\_main}.
  \end{itemize}
  \item \texttt{\_\_libc\_start\_main} (in glibc):
  \begin{itemize}
    \item Initializes the C library (locale, stdio buffers).
    \item Calls \texttt{\_\_libc\_csu\_init}, which calls functions in \texttt{.init\_array}.
    \item Calls \texttt{main(argc, argv, envp)}.
    \item Calls \texttt{exit(main\_return\_value)}.
  \end{itemize}
  \item \texttt{exit} calls functions in \texttt{.fini\_array}, then terminates the process.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Language-Specific Initialization}\label{sec:lang-specific-init}

Languages with rich runtimes perform additional initialization before \texttt{main}:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Language runtime initialization tasks}
\label{tab:lang-init}
\begin{tabular}{lp{8cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Language} & \textbf{Initialization Tasks} \\
\hline
Java & Start JVM, load bootstrap classes, initialize system class loader, run \texttt{main} \\
Go & Initialize goroutine scheduler, set up GC, run \texttt{init()} functions, call \texttt{main} \\
Rust & Call \texttt{std::rt::lang\_start}, set up allocator, run \texttt{init} functions, call \texttt{main} \\
Python & Initialize interpreter, import \texttt{site} module, run \texttt{\_\_main\_\_} \\
Node.js & Initialize V8, set up event loop, load \texttt{module} system, run entry script \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Static Initialization Order}\label{sec:static-init-order}

A subtle issue in languages with global constructors (C++, Java, D) is the \emph{static initialization order fiasco}: the order in which global objects in different translation units are initialized is not guaranteed.

\begin{definition}[Static Initialization Order Fiasco]\label{def:siof}
The \emph{static initialization order fiasco} is the problem that global objects in different translation units may be initialized in an arbitrary order. If one global object's initialization depends on another global object that has not yet been initialized, the behavior is undefined.
\end{definition}

Solutions include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Construct on first use} (Meyers' singleton): wrap the global in a function that initializes it on first call.
  \item \textbf{Nifty counters}: use a translation-unit-local counter to ensure initialization order.
  \item \textbf{Module-level initialization}: languages like Rust and Go guarantee that module-level initialization runs in dependency order.
  \item \textbf{Lazy initialization}: defer initialization until the object is first accessed.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Runtime Teardown}\label{sec:runtime-teardown}

Program termination is as complex as startup:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \texttt{main} returns.
  \item \texttt{exit} is called (explicitly or implicitly).
  \item Functions registered with \texttt{atexit} are called in reverse order.
  \item Functions in \texttt{.fini\_array} are called (destructors for global objects).
  \item Shared library finalizers are called in reverse load order.
  \item The C library flushes and closes stdio streams.
  \item The process calls \texttt{\_exit} to terminate.
\end{enumerate}

For garbage-collected languages, the runtime may or may not run finalizers during shutdown. Java runs finalizers for reachable objects; Go does not run finalizers at all during shutdown.

