\section{Runtime Code Patching and Hot Swapping}\label{sec:code-patching}

Modern runtimes sometimes modify code at runtime, either for optimization or for updating running programs.

\subsection{Inline Caching and Code Patching}\label{sec:ic-patching}

Dynamic languages use inline caches that are patched at runtime:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Before patching (generic dispatch):
  //   call generic_dispatch    // Slow path
  
  // After patching (monomorphic, type X observed):
  //   cmp [obj.type], TYPE_X   // Check type
  //   jne slow_path            // Fall back if wrong type
  //   call optimized_X         // Fast path for type X
\end{verbatim}

Code patching requires:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The code page must be made writable (\texttt{mprotect}).
  \item The patch must be atomic (to avoid races with other threads executing the same code).
  \item The instruction cache must be flushed (on architectures with separate I-cache and D-cache, e.g., ARM).
  \item The code page must be made executable again.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Hot Code Loading}\label{sec:hot-code-loading}

Some runtimes support \emph{hot code loading}: replacing the implementation of a function while the program is running. This requires:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Version management}: each compiled version of a function has a unique version number.
  \item \textbf{On-stack replacement (OSR)}: existing activations of the old version must be migrated to the new version.
  \item \textbf{State mapping}: the new version's local variables must be initialized from the old version's state.
  \item \textbf{Atomic switching}: the transition from old to new code must be atomic.
\end{itemize}

Erlang/OTP is the canonical example: processes can be upgraded to new code versions while running, with state transformation functions mapping old state to new state.

\section{Summary}\label{sec:runtime-org-summary}

The runtime organization of a compiled program encompasses the layout of its address space, the structure of its stack frames, the management of its heap, and the initialization and support services provided by the runtime system. The compiler must generate code that conforms to these conventions and, for many languages, must also provide the runtime infrastructure that implements higher-level language features.

\begin{historical}
The concept of a runtime system evolved alongside programming languages. Early compiled languages like FORTRAN and C had minimal runtimes---essentially just the C library and the operating system. As languages grew more sophisticated, so did their runtimes: Java's JVM, .NET's CLR, and the Go runtime all provide extensive services including garbage collection, concurrency, and dynamic loading. The trend toward ``thick'' runtimes has been partially reversed in recent years by languages like Rust and Zig, which minimize runtime overhead by shifting more responsibility to compile time. The tension between runtime richness and runtime minimalism remains one of the central design decisions in language implementation.
\end{historical}
